    12	# Design analysis for the fused variant
    13	# ------------------------------------
    14	# a) Behavior trace:
    15	#    The original task finds a rectangular 2xN / Nx2 palette and fills only truly enclosed
    16	#    background holes. In this fused version the palette is a connected T or L shape on the
    17	#    border, with the edge-aligned branch used for small sprites and the inward branch used for
    18	#    large sprites. Filling no longer follows flood-filled holes; it uses the convex hull of each
    19	#    sprite's border pixels. A concave or open "C/U" border therefore becomes a larger filled
    20	#    convex region in the output.
    21	# b) Complexity calibration:
    22	#    The palette keeps only two semantic classes, small and large, so the rule is visible but not
    23	#    combinatorially noisy. The same border color is intentionally reused in both branches with
    24	#    different fill colors; this prevents the task from degenerating into a single flat color map.
    25	# c) Hidden assumptions replaced:
    26	#    Old code assumed a rectangular palette and closed holes. New code assumes instead:
    27	#    - exactly one connected non-background border-touching component is the palette;
    28	#    - top/bottom palettes are T-shaped and left/right palettes are L-shaped;
    29	#    - sprite size is inferred from bbox area, with the generator keeping small <= 16 and
    30	#      large >= 25 so there is no ambiguous middle band;
    31	#    - sprites stay away from the frame and from one another, so convex-hull filling cannot
    32	#      accidentally absorb unrelated objects.
    33	# d) Placement legality:
    34	#    The generator places the palette first, then sprites with a 1-cell moat. Sprites are kept
    35	#    strictly inside the frame, and convex hulls stay inside each sprite bbox, so no overlap or
    36	#    out-of-bounds fill can occur.
    37	# e) Visual observability:
    38	#    Every sample contains a small sprite and a large sprite sharing the same border color but
    39	#    receiving different fills, so branch semantics are visible. At least one keyed sprite is a
    40	#    concave/open shape whose convex hull adds cells beyond any ordinary enclosed hole fill.
    41	# f) Visual cleanliness:
    42	#    Only three sprites are used, all monochrome and separated. The palette is the lone
    43	#    boundary-touching component, which keeps object roles legible.
    44	# g) Inferability of change:
    45	#    The color change is always justified by two visible cues: palette branch membership and
    46	#    sprite size class. The generator fixes those cues across all samples instead of relying on
    47	#    numeric color ids or hidden ordering.
    48	
